\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Task-level Dice (\%). Each column pools that task's held-out contrasts and both source modalities, plus (CHAOS, Liver-HCC) an external cross-dataset test set pooled by contrast group so that a modality tested by several external sources does not outvote one tested by fewer (\cref  {sec:suppl-persetting}); \textbf  {overall} weights tasks equally. Liver-HCC trains on a single modality (\cref  {subsec:e-setup}), so its column is cross-dataset rather than cross-contrast evidence. HD95, a secondary metric here, is reported separately in \cref  {tab:meta-hd95}. $p$: Holm-corrected one-sided sign-flip test on the macro-averaged per-case difference, stratified by task. PALETTE attains the best Dice on every task and improves significantly over every competitor.\relax }}{6}{table.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:meta}{{2}{6}{Task-level Dice (\%). Each column pools that task's held-out contrasts and both source modalities, plus (CHAOS, Liver-HCC) an external cross-dataset test set pooled by contrast group so that a modality tested by several external sources does not outvote one tested by fewer (\cref {sec:suppl-persetting}); \textbf {overall} weights tasks equally. Liver-HCC trains on a single modality (\cref {subsec:e-setup}), so its column is cross-dataset rather than cross-contrast evidence. HD95, a secondary metric here, is reported separately in \cref {tab:meta-hd95}. $p$: Holm-corrected one-sided sign-flip test on the macro-averaged per-case difference, stratified by task. PALETTE attains the best Dice on every task and improves significantly over every competitor.\relax }{table.caption.10}{}}
